answer/value "2y + x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e2e7185-13ca-551c-b2b9-a1f06717c040" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y + z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130970} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d720396-d9d8-5526-a6c9-d783c2ddfa07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130966}] :question/id #uuid "9360d1cf-9517-569c-8e77-dfd2eb8609da" :question/math-academy-id "q-162407" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x = 1$, $x = 3$, $y =-1$, $y = 1$, $z = 0$, $z = 1$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}=xz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}$, the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ can be written as\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[2pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\n$$\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "The divergence is\n$$\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F=\\frac{\\partial(xz)}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial(y^2)}{\\partial y}+\\frac{\\partial1}{\\partial z}=z+2y.$$\nThe Divergence Theorem therefore makes the missing integrand $z+2y$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134136 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02b4e90d-c970-54c3-ab06-a30ba09864e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{4}\\,z+3y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278607} {:answer/id #uuid "7ebe8543-ec3e-587e-9768-a831766e3eb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}^{y-2z}\\,{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279734} {:answer/id #uuid "aa98d390-38c9-5242-9163-03ea126a9877" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z}^{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280105} {:answer/id #uuid "acad9a07-0f5d-5457-86b1-82efec638ac7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z}^{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,2y+1\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280120} {:answer/id #uuid "ea617dc5-3c2b-587f-bfa4-9046fb77867d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}^{y-2z}\\,2y+1\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa98d390-38c9-5242-9163-03ea126a9877" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z}^{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280105} :answer-field/id #uuid "26ee8cb1-a891-5ada-b9cf-ae248d766e50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280903}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "152cbc4e-4f16-538e-9423-127439b0fd3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162408" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $y=-1,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=1,$ and the surfaces $x=y-2z$ and $x=4-{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}=x{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+xz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}+0+0 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type II solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,y-2z\\le x\\le 4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}y-2z}}}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{y}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281503 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "100640c1-bb74-5261-b07b-162818bcec64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,2xz\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278736} {:answer/id #uuid "612e775d-f4d3-5491-a568-8632b1dc20b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2y}^{2{x}^{2}+1}\\,{x}^{2}-1\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279479} {:answer/id #uuid "a3e024e7-b5ac-5532-a30e-fcebd4a57c4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2y}^{2{x}^{2}+1}\\,y+z-2\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280046} {:answer/id #uuid "bd3f43b4-3268-56c7-b7af-4bdd5b0709c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,y+z-2\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280255} {:answer/id #uuid "c001c748-078c-5145-9bb6-76a3e6bc31f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,{x}^{2}-1\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280282}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c001c748-078c-5145-9bb6-76a3e6bc31f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,{x}^{2}-1\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280282} :answer-field/id #uuid "78ab3a06-7407-5d2e-b3fb-5448f190ff8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281052}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "34056bf8-ebf0-50cf-86bc-5d67c31ea40b" :question/math-academy-id "q-162588" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ and the surfaces $z=y$ and $z={x}^{2}+y.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}=(x+y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{x}^{2}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}z) \\\\  & =1+(-2)+{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}({x}^{2}-1)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type I solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,y\\le z\\le {x}^{2}+y\\}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}-1\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}y}}}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{x}^{2}+y}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{x}^{2}-1}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281504 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fe9f3a4-5cf2-57da-bcd0-f2ef4cb40acb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,x+y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278733} {:answer/id #uuid "22082459-dc16-5814-aa58-32ad4a0572c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,2xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278901} {:answer/id #uuid "54ac7f0f-775a-50ab-b1ab-fadfeb9bb310" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}^{2}\\,3{y}^{2}+xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279362} {:answer/id #uuid "5e06d3d8-095d-5f06-bf6e-56940d9ad14d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2}^{{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,3{y}^{2}+xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279452} {:answer/id #uuid "e7f6523c-8035-585f-ad29-1d759e7d0636" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}^{2}\\,2xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7f6523c-8035-585f-ad29-1d759e7d0636" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}^{2}\\,2xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280633} :answer-field/id #uuid "070d1c83-7b76-5bd7-a3dc-6b326edc9f9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280842}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8e6de64a-d194-59f6-b620-cf3ddbd502d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-162589" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=1,$ and the surfaces $y={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and $y=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}=({y}^{3}-z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2xyz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{3}-z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2xyz) \\\\  & =0+0+2xy \\\\  & =2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}2xy\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type III solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}-1\\le x\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le z\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le y\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}2xy\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}2}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}2xy}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398562 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a60a0ff-d58d-574d-ad7b-aa68f48f45e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+y+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398564} {:answer/id #uuid "caf3aed4-56d3-57fb-9c54-0b9379ad0474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398565} {:answer/id #uuid "5e393df8-940d-5225-8bcc-4aba9a085593" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398566} {:answer/id #uuid "8ebc6e76-bfea-5200-aea9-f978a3678ba3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398567} {:answer/id #uuid "c0a5acac-f897-54fc-9f0e-b6da562e322e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+y+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398568}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0a5acac-f897-54fc-9f0e-b6da562e322e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+y+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398568} :answer-field/id #uuid "196954bd-17a6-525e-a3ea-36557f290435" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398563}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e0d14173-8029-50a6-8f00-d7853c057071" :question/math-academy-id "q-162581" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=0,$ $y=1,$ $y=2,$ $z=0,$ $z=1.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}=xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{x}^{2}z\\mathbf{k},$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[2pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}z) \\\\  & =y+2+{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+y+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}({x}^{2}+y+2)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{x}^{2}+y+2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398569 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc53f04c-d5bb-59dd-b389-8e0eefab0229" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398571} {:answer/id #uuid "af12ea26-8299-5100-8312-7691bb7b67f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-z+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398572} {:answer/id #uuid "958be202-ad70-5f65-942d-3cf053018cf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398573} {:answer/id #uuid "221c39d4-97a7-58e1-af0f-bd5bd145ae18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x+4z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398574} {:answer/id #uuid "fc84079a-9c1c-54c9-bc3c-5fd874d6a1dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-y-2z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "958be202-ad70-5f65-942d-3cf053018cf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398573} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b4c4f1b-ebdf-554c-9f5f-c8043f8e6124" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398570}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "20faa8ac-7475-55a0-94fb-35eb1571259e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162583" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=4,$ $y=-1,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}=({x}^{2}+y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-yz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+4xz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[2pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-yz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4xz) \\\\  & =2x-z+4x \\\\  & =6x-z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}(6x-z)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}6x-z}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430947 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "499cdd5b-e6c0-5bd4-bfc7-e7acaa3926fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x-z+2}\\,3{z}^{2}-4\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430922} {:answer/id #uuid "4e983312-df08-546e-9806-4e462f4e1659" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2}\\,3{z}^{2}-4\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430923} {:answer/id #uuid "688adcb4-8f82-5557-b006-90cbca149a33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2}\\,{z}^{3}-3\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430927} {:answer/id #uuid "8128a8cf-b3f9-556e-aefb-154a9c6699fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x-z+2}\\,{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430931} {:answer/id #uuid "d03cded5-b5e9-5895-a862-9145e719e5f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x-z+2}\\,{z}^{3}-3\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "499cdd5b-e6c0-5bd4-bfc7-e7acaa3926fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x-z+2}\\,3{z}^{2}-4\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430922} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ef02b27-25c8-5d51-9b89-2000755f118a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430943}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "00083281-29d7-555a-802b-bb136fd26b95" :question/math-academy-id "q-162586" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=1,$ and the surfaces $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=x-z+2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}=z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-4y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{z}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-4y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{3}) \\\\  & =0-4+3{z}^{2} \\\\  & =3{z}^{2}-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}(3{z}^{2}-4)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type III solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x-z+2\\}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(3{z}^{2}-4)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{x}^{2}}}}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}x-z+2}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}3{z}^{2}-4}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430952 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "069804e3-7c58-5f7e-b7b1-a0c2c5c704f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430911} {:answer/id #uuid "06c35222-68d1-50e1-b3be-f3859d1566f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430912} {:answer/id #uuid "165ccd61-a96d-55ca-a818-a06291f0c969" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430914} {:answer/id #uuid "661de3a9-92bc-5da3-91fa-17df7bbafc4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430925} {:answer/id #uuid "fd490bcb-15ef-5833-9df8-21e37b4504ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+yz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430940}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "069804e3-7c58-5f7e-b7b1-a0c2c5c704f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430911} :answer-field/id #uuid "89b39073-1256-589c-ae05-81365bf7916b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430942}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cd56ae3f-1187-5181-af79-1866b5188a10" :question/math-academy-id "q-162575" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=2,$ $z=1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}={x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+xyz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[2pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xyz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2y) \\\\  & =2x+xz+0 \\\\  & =2x+xz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}(2x+xz)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}2x+xz}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557238 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bc77a23-0eee-55f5-af88-ab3ad6a354e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556453} {:answer/id #uuid "4bb5a8f6-ed9a-5ef1-aebf-3359a8c0719a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y{e}^{x}+2z+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556597} {:answer/id #uuid "4c276ef4-b454-5573-8609-3f6090682ed7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556599} {:answer/id #uuid "555ab874-d0dc-5e4c-a1b1-97fc4821c9e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}+2z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556627} {:answer/id #uuid "83db7844-9dcf-556d-9a9a-daf1ca164005" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y{e}^{x}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556739}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83db7844-9dcf-556d-9a9a-daf1ca164005" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y{e}^{x}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556739} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ef39217-f0e4-532f-b400-3709c241a074" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557094}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3d423292-2025-559a-9094-6ebc6fb41834" :question/math-academy-id "q-162577" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=3,$ $y=-1,$ $y=2,$ $z=0,$ $z=4.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}=y{e}^{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{z}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[2pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{e}^{x})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3z) \\\\  & =y{e}^{x}+0+3 \\\\  & =y{e}^{x}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}(y{e}^{x}+3)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}y{e}^{x}+3}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557290 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "282ca48e-2a60-5aa5-a7ef-5e8ccb1a7c1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{y}}+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556517} {:answer/id #uuid "3a51c99c-bb63-5030-af27-f60c30a2ad6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556555} {:answer/id #uuid "493526e7-2497-5843-9007-3650fcadb2fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{1}{y}+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556592} {:answer/id #uuid "4e92e89f-6a0f-5b5c-9cc9-2174221f6a2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{1}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556603} {:answer/id #uuid "dde34e84-5b46-56e1-87f0-de42f2772090" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{y}+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556981}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e92e89f-6a0f-5b5c-9cc9-2174221f6a2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{1}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556603} :answer-field/id #uuid "19af3f29-aebd-50f6-b224-a1f92ead3b61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557081}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a809785e-b48f-5cb0-92f3-ff24733e01ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-162579" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=2,$ $y=1,$ $y=3,$ $z=1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}=x\\sqrt{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\operatorname{ln}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[2pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\sqrt{y})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\operatorname{ln}y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{1}{y}+0 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{1}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}(\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{1}{y})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{1}{y}}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557319 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eed31b1-1432-5919-b43b-eb4ed1548165" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y-z}^{1+{y}^{2}}\\,3x{z}^{2}+z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556458} {:answer/id #uuid "2a1a0b26-5443-5a61-939e-04dd145cbdfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\,x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556520} {:answer/id #uuid "36efeb09-1895-56f2-887a-74b67306458d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\,3x{z}^{2}+z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556550} {:answer/id #uuid "54b2d94d-d3b1-5e20-863e-0f3029049800" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y-z}^{1+{y}^{2}}\\,x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556625} {:answer/id #uuid "847aa225-9761-56bc-b061-f790278ac3ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y-z}^{1+{y}^{2}}\\,{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556742}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54b2d94d-d3b1-5e20-863e-0f3029049800" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y-z}^{1+{y}^{2}}\\,x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556625} :answer-field/id #uuid "498c1fb4-5fbe-531d-bc45-e785a72a6f50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557106}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e44dc84b-de4c-5508-b285-9f3d93ffdc55" :question/math-academy-id "q-162587" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $y=-1,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=2,$ and the surfaces $x=y-z$ and $x=1+{y}^{2}.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathbf{i}+x{z}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+xz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{z}^{3})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xz) \\\\  & =0+0+x \\\\  & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}x\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type II solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 2,\\,y-z\\le x\\le 1+{y}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y-z}^{1+{y}^{2}}\\,x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567466 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37ee2f7c-247b-52aa-9772-dd5afaed8cf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z+2}^{-y^{2}-z^{2}+6}\\,(6y+1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567464} {:answer/id #uuid "768baa50-a05a-5a93-8a7d-c7eafc56d215" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\,(7y+z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567472} {:answer/id #uuid "78e89c2c-64e1-5070-85f7-d9057064fd2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z+2}^{-y^{2}-z^{2}+6}\\,3y^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567474} {:answer/id #uuid "a7661633-5385-5f1b-bf44-36c47f194c2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y^{2}-z^{2}+6}^{y-2z+2}\\,3y^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567481} {:answer/id #uuid "fe4f393a-a837-557c-8ec1-f57355dc6c1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y^{2}-z^{2}+6}^{y-2z+2}\\,(6y+1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78e89c2c-64e1-5070-85f7-d9057064fd2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z+2}^{-y^{2}-z^{2}+6}\\,3y^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567474} :answer-field/id #uuid "76ffb8ed-a3fc-5061-963c-f9e84dcfaa17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567473}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "446721e6-e784-5554-b75e-8df7d632fd3e" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $y=-1$, $y=\\frac{1}{2}$, $z=0$, $z=1$, and the surfaces $x=y-2z+2$ and $x=-y^{2}-z^{2}+6$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(3xy^{2})\\,i+(xz)\\,j+(y)\\,k$, the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3xy^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y) \\\\  & =3y^{2}+0+0 \\\\  & =3y^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}3y^{2}\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type II solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\left\\{(x,y,z):\\begin{array}{l}-1\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\le z\\le 1 \\\\ y-2z+2\\le x\\le -y^{2}-z^{2}+6\\end{array}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&{∭}_{R}3y^{2}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z+2}^{-y^{2}-z^{2}+6}\\,3y^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567467 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "124580ce-b588-5d3e-9e6a-c572d58acbfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\,8x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567457} {:answer/id #uuid "87a415a9-7010-5dbc-ab45-43faaef320c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y-z+3}^{y^{2}+4}\\,(18xz^{2}+8z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567475} {:answer/id #uuid "944d0d6d-8299-5ec4-88d6-8f32d284cab1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y-z+3}^{y^{2}+4}\\,8x^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567477} {:answer/id #uuid "bdf8d52c-c2fc-57b1-943f-48958d8dfaca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y-z+3}^{y^{2}+4}\\,8x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567483} {:answer/id #uuid "ff36e153-7038-5984-9043-4c16de9c0b14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\,(18xz^{2}+8z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567491}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdf8d52c-c2fc-57b1-943f-48958d8dfaca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y-z+3}^{y^{2}+4}\\,8x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567483} :answer-field/id #uuid "e33f842f-ed58-50c9-9066-163f83156bfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567487}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4fc3af64-5048-55c7-b8cf-0b264930b564" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $y=-1$, $y=1$, $z=0$, $z=2$, and the surfaces $x=y-z+3$ and $x=y^{2}+4$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(6)\\,i+(6xz^{3})\\,j+(8xz)\\,k$, the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6xz^{3})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(8xz) \\\\  & =0+0+8x \\\\  & =8x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}8x\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type II solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\left\\{(x,y,z):\\begin{array}{l}-1\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0\\le z\\le 2 \\\\ y-z+3\\le x\\le y^{2}+4\\end{array}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&{∭}_{R}8x\\,\\text{d}V \\\\ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y-z+3}^{y^{2}+4}\\,8x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567468 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25474019-29f2-5d23-bd25-c70f93ba8fa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\,(8x+8y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567461} {:answer/id #uuid "2df2f35a-fd7f-58bd-9a38-5d67fd7e04a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{4}^{x^{2}+z^{2}+2}\\,(10xy+24y^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567463} {:answer/id #uuid "51466662-d135-556e-87ab-060e7701ea44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x^{2}+z^{2}+2}^{4}\\,18xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567469} {:answer/id #uuid "75383faf-100a-5fce-9614-49109b1eda2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x^{2}+z^{2}+2}^{4}\\,(10xy+24y^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567471} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb607ac-2c00-52b0-a185-aea08347a312" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\,18xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51466662-d135-556e-87ab-060e7701ea44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x^{2}+z^{2}+2}^{4}\\,18xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567469} :answer-field/id #uuid "99fbac14-39f5-5ce3-9082-f2eb9f660e07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567479}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50d6274c-6dfe-5f3c-a4fd-5791e674d566" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=0$, $x=\\frac{1}{2}$, $z=0$, $z=1$, and the surfaces $y=x^{2}+z^{2}+2$ and $y=4$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(8y^{3}-8z)\\,i+(8z)\\,j+(18xyz)\\,k$, the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8y^{3}-8z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(8z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(18xyz) \\\\  & =0+0+18xy \\\\  & =18xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}18xy\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type III solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\left\\{(x,y,z):\\begin{array}{l}0\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\le z\\le 1 \\\\ x^{2}+z^{2}+2\\le y\\le 4\\end{array}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&{∭}_{R}18xy\\,\\text{d}V \\\\ & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x^{2}+z^{2}+2}^{4}\\,18xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567480 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2419d35f-c467-5108-ab90-a73a47d7492b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{3}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{x^{2}+y+2}\\,(6x^{2}-8)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567459} {:answer/id #uuid "242b1789-1198-5bda-aa0a-fec59581a470" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{3}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{x^{2}+y+2}\\,(6y+6z-14)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567460} {:answer/id #uuid "8de41433-0b6f-54e3-9b49-d8040860ad29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{3}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2y}^{2x^{2}+3}\\,(6y+6z-14)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567476} {:answer/id #uuid "98ad758f-63e8-5860-b6c8-6443e4b323c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{3}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{x^{2}+y+2}\\,12xz\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567478} {:answer/id #uuid "c213557d-6b75-5e6c-bdd5-775c247bf3e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{3}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2y}^{2x^{2}+3}\\,(6x^{2}-8)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567485}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2419d35f-c467-5108-ab90-a73a47d7492b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{3}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{x^{2}+y+2}\\,(6x^{2}-8)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567459} :answer-field/id #uuid "224d0646-145b-5963-b7fa-9f4bfa64c486" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567458}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9be79e17-8b58-5c1b-806e-44da9409c071" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=0$, $x=\\frac{3}{2}$, $y=0$, $y=1$, and the surfaces $z=y$ and $z=x^{2}+y+2$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(6x+6y)\\,i+(-14y)\\,j+(6x^{2}z)\\,k$, the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6x+6y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-14y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(6x^{2}z) \\\\  & =6+(-14)+6x^{2} \\\\  & =6x^{2}-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}(6x^{2}-8)\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type I solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\left\\{(x,y,z):\\begin{array}{l}0\\le x\\le \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ y\\le z\\le x^{2}+y+2\\end{array}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&{∭}_{R}(6x^{2}-8)\\,\\text{d}V \\\\ & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{3}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{x^{2}+y+2}\\,(6x^{2}-8)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567484 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42f53cd9-452a-5b7a-90b0-7cf01f8c51e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x^{2}+2}^{6}\\,(6z^{3}-20)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567465} {:answer/id #uuid "5f378c1e-cb4b-520c-b85b-e9642eaa693a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x^{2}+2}^{x-z+6}\\,(6z^{3}-20)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567470} {:answer/id #uuid "b4517b2b-d5ce-589e-b0b6-535bfba07d4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x^{2}+2}^{6}\\,(18z^{2}-26)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567482} {:answer/id #uuid "f342e2b8-7df2-5134-a17a-98c7501cee4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x^{2}+2}^{x-z+6}\\,6z^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567488} {:answer/id #uuid "fa44c4cb-442e-5ee1-a900-ce805cf6055e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x^{2}+2}^{x-z+6}\\,(18z^{2}-26)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa44c4cb-442e-5ee1-a900-ce805cf6055e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x^{2}+2}^{x-z+6}\\,(18z^{2}-26)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567489} :answer-field/id #uuid "27cfce2e-64df-5654-aecd-ec39a7902c99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567462}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "be69cf6b-98de-5d36-ac17-56f847206dd5" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=0$, $x=1$, $z=0$, $z=1$, and the surfaces $y=x^{2}+2$ and $y=x-z+6$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(6z)\\,i+(-26y)\\,j+(6z^{3})\\,k$, the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-26y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(6z^{3}) \\\\  & =0+(-26)+18z^{2} \\\\  & =18z^{2}-26.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}(18z^{2}-26)\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type III solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\left\\{(x,y,z):\\begin{array}{l}0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 0\\le z\\le 1 \\\\ x^{2}+2\\le y\\le x-z+6\\end{array}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&{∭}_{R}(18z^{2}-26)\\,\\text{d}V \\\\ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x^{2}+2}^{x-z+6}\\,(18z^{2}-26)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Flux Across a Closed Surface as a Triple Integral"}]}
